# Target C: exact vacancy pinning and the scope of one-deficit classification

Date: 2026-10-07. Base `bf288c82e8a62466d5580025c94e750a177e63e2`;
main `dcc21aa879b61662318bceb9aeb174792e074eb0`; state
`2026-10-06.audit.71`. Classification: **proved candidate awaiting independent
audit** for Sections 1--4; **conditional** for the curved-wall application.
C-II and Level I remain open. No promotion is attempted.

The implication under attack is

`arbitrary one-deficit limiting cloud -> vacancy chart or another usable defect
-> finite triangle / full-source exchange -> canonical contradiction`.

This report does not supply the first arrow. It proves that *every* vacancy
chart has a zero static infinitesimal cone and gives an O(theta) reduction
for all such charts. It also proves that unrestricted flat-window vacancy
classification, for all window sizes, is equivalent to a strong-solidity
statement. Equality in Oler must not be substituted for this missing theorem.

## 1. Objects and explicit positive equilibrium

Let u=(1,0), v=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2), Lambda=Zu+Zv, and

`H_M={iu+jv: max(|i|,|j|,|i+j|)<=M}`.

Write B_M for its 6M boundary lattice sites, P=H_M minus B_M, and
N=|P|=3M^2-3M+1. Fix M>=2 and *any* h in P. The moving chart is
P minus {h}; all B_M sites are pinned. This is an auxiliary target chart,
not an assertion about an arbitrary old source. Include every surviving
unit-neighbour edge with a moving endpoint.

Give each edge weight one, and add one to each unit edge of the hexagon
of the six neighbours of h. Edges with both endpoints pinned may be
omitted. Every included weight is at least one.

**Lemma 1.** These weights equilibrate every moving vertex.

For p not adjacent to h, its six neighbour gradients sum to zero, and
no incident weight changes. At a neighbour p=h+e, deletion of edge ph
changes its old zero resultant by -e. The two neighbour-ring gradients
at p sum to e: their endpoints are the preceding and succeeding unit
directions around h. The added weights therefore restore zero. Every
neighbour of every interior lattice site lies in H_M, including when h
is one row from a side or an inner radial corner. This is an exact
integer vector identity in axial coordinates.

Consequently a first-order lower-feasible field with pinned boundary
has zero variation on *every* surviving unit edge: its nonnegative row
values have positive weighted sum zero. There is no unsupported
extension of the canonical ZR/ZF theorem to isolates.

## 2. Pinned bar rank and the zero cone

**Lemma 2.** The surviving unit-edge matrix has full moving-column rank,
2(N-1). Hence the static lower-feasibility cone is {0}.

Triangulate the full hexagonal window into elementary lattice triangles.
Deleting h and its incident triangles removes its regular unit-neighbour
hexagon. The surviving triangles have connected edge-adjacency. Indeed,
if h has radius at most M-2, this removes an interior hexagonal hole.
If h has radius M-1, that hole meets the outer boundary in either one
unit edge or two adjacent unit edges, a single connected arc; it cannot
join opposite outer sides for M>=2. The remaining polygonal region has
connected interior. Its elementary triangulation therefore has connected
dual graph. At least one surviving triangle has a full boundary edge.
Every remaining interior vertex belongs to a surviving triangle (at most
two of its six incident triangles meet h).

A zero bar-variation field on one nondegenerate triangle is an infinitesimal
Euclidean rigid motion. Edge-adjacent triangles have the same rigid motion,
since they agree at two distinct common vertices. Dual connectivity makes
the field one rigid motion on all surviving triangles. The fixed endpoints
of a surviving outer boundary edge kill that motion. Thus every moving
velocity is zero. Combine this with Lemma 1 to obtain the zero strut cone.

This proof includes off-axis and boundary-row vacancies; it is not limited
to the central vacancy used in Worker 1 checkpoint 15.

## 3. Finite local isolation and perturbed-wall rate reduction

Let R be the fixed finite unit-edge row matrix, with rows
`(p-q) dot (delta_p-delta_q)` in moving coordinates; pinned displacements
are zero. Lemma 1 gives a vector lambda>0 with R^T lambda=0, and Lemma 2
gives full column rank.

For a feasible finite displacement delta with the static pins,

`(R delta)_e >= -|delta_p-delta_q|^2/2 >= -c ||delta||^2`.

The positive weighted sum of these linear row values is zero. Thus each
row value is also bounded above by c' ||delta||^2. A left inverse for R
gives `||delta|| <= c'' ||delta||^2`. All sufficiently small feasible
displacements are therefore zero. This is local isolation, not an
unrestricted rearrangement theorem.

Now replace the pinned boundary by q(theta)=q(0)+O(theta), including the
specified curved unit wall, and suppose the moving placements converge
after relabeling to this vacancy chart. The same old unit inequalities give

`R delta >= -c(theta+||delta||^2)`.

Multiply by the same *static* positive stress and use its zero moving
resultant to get the matching upper row bounds. Full rank then yields

`||delta|| <= c'(theta+||delta||^2)`.

Since delta tends to zero, absorb the quadratic term. Hence

`||delta||=O(theta)`.

Every such sequence admits a subsequential bounded first velocity, and
that velocity satisfies the complete inhomogeneous first-row system with
the prescribed wall velocity. This rules out a hidden larger-than-theta
scale for *all vacancy charts*. It does not classify that bounded polytope,
force finite equality contacts, or identify arbitrary one-deficit clouds
with a vacancy chart. Constants depend on fixed M and h; no uniform
curvature onset in M is asserted.

## 4. Exact equivalence for the stronger proposed flat classification

Define FV as follows: for every M>=2, every unit-separated set X of N-1
points strictly inside conv(H_M), also unit-separated from B_M, is P with
one lattice site omitted. Define SS as follows: every packing obtained by
replacing a finite set of centers in Lambda minus one site by the same
number of centers is congruent to Lambda minus one site.

**Proposition 3.** FV and SS are equivalent (for unlabeled center sets).

*SS implies FV.* Append all lattice sites outside the open hexagonal
window to X. This is a packing. To check the only new pairs, let x in X
and y be an exterior lattice site, and let xy first cross a unit boundary
segment ab at z=(1-t)a+tb. The weighted-square identity and clearance
from a,b give

`|x-z|^2 >= 1-t(1-t) >= 3/4`,
`|y-z|^2 >= 1-t(1-t) >= 3/4`.

Because z is on xy, |x-y|>=sqrt(3). A boundary-vertex exit instead gives
|x-y|>=2. Boundary sites themselves were separately guarded. Thus the
appended infinite packing is a finite same-cardinality rearrangement of
Lambda with one interior site removed. SS says it is congruent to that
punctured lattice. Agreement with Lambda outside a bounded set forces
its underlying congruent lattice to be Lambda: a large intact lattice
patch fixes the unit directions and the translation coset. Hence X is
the required lattice-vacancy set.

*FV implies SS.* Enclose all changed old centers, all inserted centers,
and the original omitted site strictly inside a sufficiently large H_M.
Its boundary and exterior lattice sites are unchanged. Finite cardinality
conservation gives exactly N-1 centers in that window. FV identifies
these centers as a single lattice vacancy. The resulting whole packing
is Lambda minus a site, congruent by a lattice translation to the original.

This is a theorem-scope diagnostic, not a disproof of the weaker
triangle-forcing wall claim. FV is much stronger than the needed endpoint.
A nonlattice one-deficit cloud might still contain a unit triangle or allow
a contact-loss exchange. Those weaker alternatives should be attacked.

## 5. Primary-source check and limits

A. Bezdek, K. Bezdek and R. Connelly, *Finite and Uniform Stability of
Sphere Packings*, Discrete Comput. Geom. 20 (1998), 111--130, Theorem
2.8.1 and Remark 2.8.1, printed page 125, prove uniform stability of the
triangular disk packing with one disk removed and explicitly distinguish
it from strong solidity. The remark records the Euclidean strong-solidity
case as open **in that paper**. No assertion of its present literature
status, and no global solidity theorem, is imported.

Primary authors' copy: https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~connelly/pdf/10.1007_PL00009374.pdf .
The exact normalized FV/SS equivalence above is proved here independently;
the citation explains why local stability must not silently be used as FV.
Centers in that paper have separation 2r; here r=1/2. The current curved
wall moves, unlike the fixed exterior in SS.

## 6. Exact computation and failed release search

`research/verify_target_c_flat_one_vacancy_pinning_2026_10_07.py` checks the
integer equilibrium formula and an exact triangle-propagation pinned-rank
certificate for *every* interior vacancy, M=2,...,10: 999 vacancies and
178002 moving-vertex equilibria. Every propagation step uses a triangle
with axial determinant +1 or -1. Saved output is
`research/target_c_flat_one_vacancy_pinning_certificate_2026_10_07.json`.
The all-M proof is Sections 1--3, not extrapolation from these checks.

Exploratory C2 searches at M=3,4,5,6 for the inherited boundary-row
vacancy/root chart found no positive reconstructed second margin. These
are nonexhaustive floating searches, not impossibility certificates; no
universal packet nonexistence is claimed. Search failure does not remove
the contact-budget, moving-wall, remote-packet or arbitrary-cloud routes.

## 7. Frontier effect and next exact theorem

Canonical dependencies for a future exchange are audit.64's
C-CONTACT-BUDGET-DIAMETER-EXCHANGE-ENDPOINT and audit.63's
C-ONE-VERTEX-CONVEX-WALL-SHIELD. Neither constructs a packet. Audit.71's
selected stress bounds remain at their exact full-chart scopes.

Worker-1 checkpoint 15 remains unreviewed; its central-vacancy finite
sorting classification is not used as a premise here. The new static
pinning/rate lemmas have independent proofs and concern auxiliary charts.
No old source coordinate, contact isolate, actual diameter, or source
cardinality is changed by this report.

Next exact obligation: obtain a finite triangle or a full-source winning
exchange for the bounded first-row systems of *noncentral* vacancy charts,
or establish a weaker arbitrary-cloud defect theorem that bypasses FV.
General limiting clouds, arbitrary macroscopic-container transfer, paired
exterior-cap replacement and full-core face-coupled finite moves all remain
open. Preserve this candidate; do not self-promote it.
